<DocsLayout title="Machine trust — Tether CLI" description="Signed machine records, the local trust list, and how to trust, untrust and remove machines.">
  <section>
    <p class="eyebrow">CONCEPTS</p>
    <h1>Machine trust</h1>
    <p>Anyone who can push to your sync repo can edit a manifest. So a line in a manifest grants no trust. Instead, each machine signs a record of the packages it installed. Each machine decides for itself which machine keys it trusts.</p>
    <TrustDiagram />

    <h2>Signed machine records</h2>
    <p>Each machine has an SSH signing key in <code>~/.tether/signing_key</code>. Tether makes it on <code>tether init</code>, or on the first sync after an upgrade from 1.x. Tether signs this machine's record, <code>machines/&lt;id&gt;.json</code>, in <code>machines/&lt;id&gt;.json.sig</code>. A sync signs it again when its content changed, or when the signed copy is 60 minutes old. The record lists the machine's packages and versions, its profile and its OS.</p>
    <p>Tether also signs its commits in the personal sync repo. Commit signatures are an audit trail only. They do not grant trust.</p>

    <h2>The trust list</h2>
    <p>Each machine keeps its own trust list in <code>~/.tether/trusted_keys</code>. The list never syncs. It starts with this machine's own key.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>A package installs on its own only when a trusted, signed record lists it</li>
      <li>Trust is transitive. A signed record lists every package its machine installed. So when you trust a machine, you also trust those packages, whoever installed them. A package you rejected on this machine stays blocked</li>
      <li>A sync adds manifest lines only from this machine's record and the records it trusts</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="trust-a-machine">Trust a machine</h2>
    <p>A key that signs a new machine's record waits in the Inbox as "new machine key". Before you trust it, compare its fingerprint. On the new machine, read its id and full fingerprint:</p>
    <Code code="tether machines show" />
    <p>On each of your other machines, trust it with that fingerprint:</p>
    <Code code={`tether machines trust work-laptop --fingerprint SHA256:...
# or
tether packages approve machine:work-laptop --expect SHA256:...`} />
    <ul>
      <li>Tether trusts the key only if the fingerprint matches</li>
      <li>Without <code>--fingerprint</code>, Tether shows the current fingerprint and asks. This works in a terminal only</li>
      <li>When this machine does not have the signed record yet, <code>machines trust</code> pulls the sync repo first. If the record is still missing, run <code>tether sync</code> on that machine</li>
      <li>On the dashboard Machines tab, press <code>a</code> on a machine's card to trust its key, or <code>x</code> to untrust it. Both ask first</li>
    </ul>
    <p>To stop trusting a machine:</p>
    <Code code="tether machines untrust work-laptop" />

    <h2 id="fleet-commands">Fleet commands</h2>
    <p>From one machine, you can change your other machines. Add <code>--on</code> with machine ids or hostnames, separated by commas, or <code>all</code>:</p>
    <Code code={`tether packages uninstall npm:typescript --on mini,studio
tether packages install brew:ripgrep --on mini
tether machines profile set server --on mini
tether machines trust new-laptop --fingerprint SHA256:... --on all`} />
    <ul>
      <li>Tether signs the command with this machine's key and pushes it to the personal sync repo. Each machine runs it on its next sync</li>
      <li>A machine runs a command only when it trusts the key that signed it. Trust already lets a machine's record install packages on your other machines, so trust also lets it send commands</li>
      <li>Each command has a number and expires after 7 days. A machine never runs a command twice, and does not run an expired command</li>
      <li><code>install</code> runs the same checks as <code>tether packages install</code>. A malicious package, a rejected one or one that waits in the Inbox does not install</li>
      <li><code>uninstall --on</code> uninstalls the package on those machines and stops their syncs from installing it again. Profiles do not change</li>
      <li><code>machines trust --on</code> needs the fingerprint. Compare it on the new machine first. With it, you trust a new machine on all your machines from one machine</li>
      <li>Tether refuses a machine that it does not trust, and a machine on 2.0.1 or earlier, which cannot run commands</li>
      <li><code>machines profile set --on</code> refuses a profile that you created here but did not sync yet. Run <code>tether sync</code> first</li>
      <li><code>tether machines show &lt;MACHINE&gt;</code> lists the commands that wait for that machine, and the results it reported: applied, failed or expired. The daemon on the target shows a notification when it runs commands</li>
      <li>Team and collab repos never carry commands</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The dashboard sends the same commands. On the Machines tab, open another machine's details. They list its pending commands, its results and its packages. Press <code>x</code> to uninstall the selected package there, <code>i</code> to install one, <code>p</code> to set its profile, or <code>c</code> to cancel a pending command. Press <code>A</code> on a card to trust that machine's key here and on every machine that runs commands. On the Packages tab, <code>x</code> and <code>i</code> ask which machines when other machines can take the change. Each question names the machines and warns when one has not synced for a day.</p>
    <p>A cancel can come too late. A machine that pulled the command before the cancel still runs it. Anyone who can push to the sync repo can also restore a cancelled command until it expires, because its signature stays valid.</p>

    <h2>Protection against changed records</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Changed key</strong>: when a trusted machine signs with a different key, Tether warns you and holds the new key in the Inbox as "key changed". It does not trust that record until you approve the new key</li>
      <li><strong>Failed signature</strong>: Tether ignores a record that fails its signature and warns: "Record for &lt;id&gt; fails its signature"</li>
      <li><strong>Replay</strong>: each record has a generation that grows with every save. An older signed record from the same key, such as one from git history, does not count</li>
      <li><strong>Renamed machines</strong>: trust never moves to another machine id on its own. A renamed machine waits in the Inbox as a new machine</li>
      <li><strong>Your own record</strong>: this machine builds its record from <code>~/.tether/machine.json</code>, not from the repo copy</li>
    </ul>
    <p><code>tether machines list</code> and the dashboard Machines cards show each record as a sync reads it. A record that a sync ignores shows "replayed (ignored)" or "signature failed (ignored)".</p>

    <h2 id="old-machine-ids">Old machine ids</h2>
    <p>Builds before random machine ids used the hostname as the id. Tether marks a record that may be an old id of this machine. This is only a guess. Check that no other machine uses that hostname before you remove it.</p>
    <p><code>tether machines list</code> and <code>tether status</code> show the command that removes it. On the dashboard Machines tab, press <code>D</code>, then <code>y</code>.</p>
    <Code code="tether machines remove <old-id>" />
    <p><code>machines remove</code> also removes the machine's key from the trust list on this machine.</p>

    <h2>Limits</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Anyone with a machine's <code>~/.tether/signing_key</code> can sign records as that machine. Protect it like an SSH key</li>
      <li>Machines on 1.x have no signing key. Their records grant no trust</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</DocsLayout>
